WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.680
- On your mark, get set, go!

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.310
- [Narrator] During the
late '80s and early '90s,

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.710
there was an epic kids' game
show called "Double Dare"

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:10.920
where kids got rewarded for being messy.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.280
Today, it's back on the air

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.730
and all along, there's
been one guy in charge.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.380
Meet the man that got down and dirty

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.410
for seven years straight

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.340
on the messiest game show in America.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.177
- Actually, it was eight.

00:00:20.177 --> 00:00:21.010
- [Offscreen Interviewer] Eight, okay.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:21.922
- Okay.

00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:22.755
(laughs)

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.172
(rock music)

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.970
My name is Marc Summers,
and I was the original host

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.210
of the trivia game show

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.360
and very messy game show, "Double Dare."

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.290
"Double Dare" is a show
where we ask questions.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:36.150
If you don't know the answer,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.360
you can dare the other team.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.030
They can double dare you back.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.845
- We'll take a physical challenge.

00:00:40.845 --> 00:00:41.678
(cheering)

00:00:41.678 --> 00:00:42.970
- [Marc] If you don't answer
a question on our show,

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.490
you have to take the physical challenge,

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.810
which could include whipped cream, slime,

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.040
or some messy substance.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.370
And that's how you get points.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:50.470
Ultimately, what you want to do

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.020
is go to the obstacle course,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.410
run all eight obstacles
in 60 seconds or less,

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.847
and win lots of money and prizes.

00:00:55.847 --> 00:00:58.097
(cheering)

00:01:00.523 --> 00:01:01.680
(swiping)

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:02.940
So the first day I get on the set,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.880
and I walk out and there's
an obstacle course,

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.600
which I hadn't been told about,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.200
and people are dumping whipped cream

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.490
and then this green substance

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.323
and I said, "What is that?"

00:01:10.323 --> 00:01:11.578
And they said, "That's the slime."

00:01:11.578 --> 00:01:12.662
(bell ringing)

00:01:12.662 --> 00:01:14.360
(cheering)

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.990
Back in the day, it was vanilla pudding,

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.090
applesauce, and green food coloring.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.000
Now, Nickelodeon pays some company

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.610
an exorbitant amount of
money to create the slime.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:24.130
It still has to be edible

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.620
because if it gets in a kid's mouth,

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.940
they have to be able to
digest it without any problem.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.310
Back in the early days,
when we started in 1986,

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.983
I didn't know about the mess.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.070
I had been diagnosed

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.090
with something called
Obsessive Compulsive Disorder.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.116
So from '86 until about
'94 when we did the show,

00:01:40.116 --> 00:01:42.800
there was this feeling of
being uncomfortable at times

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.140
around all the mess and slime.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.360
And that's how I found
out what I had had a name.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.320
This is the most disgusting thing.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.550
I'm gonna step back
here just a little bit.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.780
We were referring to the slime as gak.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.460
What we found out after the fact

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.020
was that gak was the
slime term for heroin.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.050
Nickelodeon puts out a product called Gak

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.771
and after the fact, didn't realize that,

00:02:01.771 --> 00:02:04.240
oops, guess we should've
done our homework.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.216
- Gak is great stuff.

00:02:06.216 --> 00:02:07.882
- Oozy.

00:02:07.882 --> 00:02:08.915
- Ugh.

00:02:08.915 --> 00:02:10.320
(laughing)

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.800
- [Marc] One thing that
people would be surprised

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:12.633
to know about the show is

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:14.230
we would test physical challenges

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.540
and bring in kids from different schools

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.960
in the evenings afterwards,

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.670
and if we couldn't
accomplish them three times,

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.280
it never made air.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.360
We're not experimenting.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.734
It's gotta be physically
possible to accomplish.

00:02:24.734 --> 00:02:26.790
It was every kid's dream

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.279
and almost every adult's dream now

00:02:28.279 --> 00:02:30.480
to be on "Double Dare."

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.070
Back in the day, when
we were doing the show,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.460
I was havin' the best time of my life.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.010
(laughing)

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.240
I've been trying to convince Nickelodeon

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.060
for the last 10 years
to bring this show back

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.210
and the answer was always
"No, no, no, no, no."

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.320
I got an email that said, "Call us."

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.737
And they said, "We're bringin' it back,

00:02:45.737 --> 00:02:47.047
"but you won't be the host,

00:02:47.047 --> 00:02:48.480
"you'll be the exec producer."

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.904
And I said, "Well, who you gonna hire?"

00:02:49.904 --> 00:02:52.330
And they said, "We found an influencer."

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.334
And I said, "I have no
idea what that means."

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:55.167
(record scratches)

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:57.540
(crickets chirping)

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:58.720
So they told me about this young lady

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.970
by the name of Liza Koshy.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:00.847
And I went online and I went,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:04.700
"Oh my gosh, she's like a
22-year-old Lucille Ball."

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.880
Passing the baton was easy,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.620
simply because at 67,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.530
to be on Nickelodeon and hosting a show,

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.700
would be a bit ridiculous.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.370
And I still have a huge say-so
because as exec producer,

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:16.860
I'm able to guide her and teach her

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.520
and she takes direction extremely well.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.453
So it's kind of a marriage made in heaven.

00:03:20.453 --> 00:03:23.080
(cheering)

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.880
People come up to me on a daily basis

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.880
and say, "Oh my gosh, I
grew up watching you."

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.610
And the fun part here is
when we're at "Double Dare"

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:30.820
shooting in Los Angeles,

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:32.510
the back two rows are grownups.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:33.997
And when I walk up, I always say,

00:03:33.997 --> 00:03:35.540
"This is my audience," and they applaud.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.330
The weird thing is that these people

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.400
who are now in their 40s
have kids the same age

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.313
they were when they
started to watch the show.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:44.470
I'm honored and flattered

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.170
to have brought so many good memories

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.050
to a couple of generations of people

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.607
who look back on Double Dare.

00:03:49.607 --> 00:03:51.610
And when people come
up and say nice things,

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.937
I just say, "Thank you
for even remembering."

00:03:59.893 --> 00:04:03.060
(digital bell chimes)

